## Build and run

- **&gt;** Build and run the desktop version, from `examples/a1-opengl-cube/`:

  ```
  cmake -S . -B build
  cmake --build build
  ./build/project --speed 0.3 --tilt 40
  ```

  You should see the rotating cube in a window. Check that the options `--speed` and `--tilt` change its motion.

- **&gt;** Build the web version, with the Emscripten SDK activated:

  ```
  emcmake cmake -S . -B build_web
  cmake --build build_web
  ```

  The directory `build_web/` then contains `index.html`, `project.js` and `project.wasm`: the same files as `web/`.

- **&gt;** Show the slide through a web server, from the root of the repository:

  ```
  python3 -m http.server
  ```

  and open `http://localhost:8000/examples/a1-opengl-cube/slides.html`.

- **&gt;** Click in the cube, then press the right arrow: check that the slide changes all the same.

## In lhtml-site

In lhtml-site, the same frame is written by the macro `demo::`, in a slide whose layout places the text on the
left and the demo on the right (see [the slide in the example talk](../../decks/lhtml-site/html/04_program/index.html)):

```
{% raw %}{% set layout = 'side' %}

= Rotating cube

media::
demo::../_demo/a1/index.html?speed=0.2&tilt=25
::{% endraw %}
```

